# The Gambler / சூதாடி
Tamil title: சூதாடி

“I had two spades and the high jack. I decided to try the hand and bid 220. As ill luck would have it, a club manila lay there as a shake...”

“Did the opposing hand have the jack of clubs?”

“Yes. Why ask? It was the club jack, and he led it at once.”

“Damn! That was his first lead?”

“Yes, sir. Otherwise I could have discarded the club manila somewhere. What could I do? I played the manila.”

“Would he let you avoid it? Then what?”

“Then? I had the high jack, remember. He had none of that suit, only an ace. He discarded it and I failed to notice. I led the jack, and that was all. He cut it with a ‘leaf,’ the eighth card, and the jack went too.”

“You fool. Why did you not lead trumps at the beginning?”

“Did I not lead them? I miscounted and thought the lower trumps had already fallen.”

“Excellent. You counted wrong, held a shake manila and still bid 220. Go away. How could you avoid losing money? And you call yourself a player?”

“Be quiet. Do you know the hand I held last week? It was worse. I had no manila in trumps, only one shake manila of another suit, yet I won. I led trumps and, pat, the lower manila fell. My partner held the manila shake. I led another trump leaf.”

“Why?”

“Was the trump ace not still outside?”

“Sinner! You held neither manila nor ace in trumps and still bid the game? Where did you get such courage?”

“I did not merely bid. I won. When I led the trump leaf, he took it with the ace. His partner threw down the jack upon it. Do you know which jack? The jack matching the shake manila in my hand. That settled it. He never recovered after that trick. Our shake manila survived, and we won the game.

“Sometimes even a terrible hand wins; sometimes a rock-solid hand loses.”

“True. Shall we play four games?”

“Only the two of us?”

“Yes. Two-handed 304 is tremendous fun.”

“What stakes?”

“Half an anna and one anna.”

“No. A quarter and half anna are enough.”

“Very well. Deal. We can play four games before they return.”

This conversation occurred in the motor shed beside the entrance of a bungalow. The speakers were Balu, driver to the bungalow’s owner Parthasarathi Mudaliar, and Moses, driver to Muthusami Iyer, who had come to visit the Mudaliar.

Inside, the Iyer spoke with the Mudaliar. In the motor shed, a game of 304 began.

In quick order Moses lost two quarter-annas. Besides the half anna lost in money, two cigarettes were consumed while he tried to calm the anger of defeat. That was an additional loss; his money went up in smoke as well.

Balu was delighted. Moses was worried.

Neither heard the Iyer’s voice. Only when he sounded the motor horn did Moses leap up in alarm and run. The Iyer was angry. Moses hurried to the car.

“Donkey! What is this? I have been shouting and shouting. Where were you?”

“In the shed.”

“What business did you have there?”

“Our car battery has been troubling us, sir. I was attending to it.”

“Did he tell you playing 304 would repair the battery?”

Moses scratched his head. Somehow the Iyer had learned they were playing cards. Moses noticed the gardener’s boy standing nearby with a mischievous look. That fellow had stirred the trouble. Moses decided to deal with him later and pretended to inspect the car.

The Iyer climbed in and the car moved. He began speaking.

“Fool! How will you ever prosper while this gambling mind remains in you? Whichever way one looks, it is always gambling with you. How can you be worthy and honest? How can a gambling fellow possess integrity?”

He continued abusing Moses in the guise of offering advice.

What could Moses reply? He was angry, but he was the driver and the other man the master. What could anger accomplish? He directed all of it at the driver of a two-bullock cart approaching from the opposite direction.

“Have you no sense, dog? Why drive in the middle of the road? All of you have become too arrogant. Because a bundle of straw that no one wanted at six annas now sells for one and a half rupees, you no longer know where your head and feet are!”

Moses’s words suited not merely the cart driver but the Iyer seated inside the car.

The Iyer’s Hamsa Mark soap had once lain unwanted. Wartime demand made soap that had sold at six annas a dozen fetch six annas for one cake. That was how the Iyer acquired both Moses and the motor car.

Moses knew it, and that was why he abused the cart driver. The Iyer cleared his throat. What else could he do? Could he ask, “Moses, did you keep me in mind while abusing that man?”

“Enough, leave him. These villagers are always like this. Drive on,” he said.

Moses felt as delighted as though he had won a double game.

After travelling some distance, the car stopped before another house. The Iyer ordered the horn sounded. After two or three blasts, another of his friends hurried out with one arm in his coat and climbed into the car.

The car left, and conversation began inside.

Muthusami Iyer said, “Seenu, do you know the news?”

“Did you finish it?”

“How could I fail? Everything depends upon the way one presents the matter.”

“Mudaliar is extremely cautious. He will not be caught easily.”

“Do I not know his hand? Did I not possess the trump with which to cut it?”

“Tell me what happened. Did he truly get caught?”

“I began by asking whether he wanted Tata shares.”

“A clever opening. Had you begun by saying you wanted his shares, he would not have yielded.”

“The fool would ruin the trump in his own hand and then stare. Should I go and ask whether he will sell me his Lakshmiraj shares?”

“You need no instruction. So you began with Tata. Then?”

“He said: ‘Tata is all you know because it is famous. Do you imagine every other share is dirt?’”

“Oh! So he began laying a foundation for praising Lakshmiraj.”

“He did. Did the idiot realize I was demolishing the foundation as he laid it?

“I said: ‘Mudaliar, Tata Company shares are like sovereigns. One cannot say the same of others. But you will naturally speak differently because...’ and drew out the sentence.

“Mudaliar grew angry at once. ‘Then do you say Lakshmiraj shares are not worth a copper?’ he snapped.

“I said: ‘Do not be angry. My mouth is somewhat blunt. Before leaving home, I told myself I should not open it. If I spoke, Mudaliar might become angry with me without realizing I labour only for the truth. But then my heart rebuked me: how can I hide what I know? What if I gain, or you suffer loss? You are my childhood friend. That is why I began this talk.

“‘Your anger is natural, for I hear you possess fifty or a hundred Lakshmiraj shares.’

“‘I have five hundred, man. What of it? Think of Lakshmiraj as a liquor shop if you please. I know its value,’ he said even more angrily.”

Seenu observed, “Yes, he is stubborn.”

“Forget his grip and argument; listen to what followed. I mocked him: ‘Mudaliar, do you imagine its value can never decline merely because its name contains both Lakshmi and Raj?’ That raised his anger further.”

“You played a low trump to bring down the opponent’s jack.”

“Listen. He asked, ‘What defect do you now find in Lakshmiraj? Seenu stood on his head yesterday urging me to buy it.’ Only then did he introduce your name.”

“See! Until then he had not mentioned me at all.”

“Did I immediately show him the telegram?”

“Which telegram?”

“The one that came yesterday saying my mother-in-law was unwell. Did I not show it to you?”

“Yes, but what did it have to do with this?”

“That, my man, was the trump jack. Listen. Mudaliar praised Lakshmiraj extravagantly. I patiently heard everything. Then I said, ‘Mudaliar, look at this,’ and extended the telegram.

“He read it and his face went pale.”

“Why?”

“Why? Did you not read what the telegram contained correctly?”

“Your mother-in-law...”

“Yes, she was in danger. I knew it, and you knew it. But what did he see? What words appeared in the telegram?

“‘Lakshmiraj sinking,’ meaning Lakshmiraj’s life was leaving her; she was sinking. My nephew had sent it. How would Mudaliar know the trick? He took Lakshmiraj to mean the shares. Imagine his condition when he read ‘sinking.’

“‘Is that so?’ he asked in fear.

“I had instructed my nephew. I told Mudaliar: ‘I too had thought of buying Lakshmiraj shares, especially because you had bought them. Would you purchase without knowing? I planned to buy and hold the same. Then yesterday this telegram arrived.’

“He read it nine times in a minute. What could he do? He threw it upon the table and began sadly saying, ‘The fellows deceived me. They tempted me by saying Lakshmiraj was immovable as stone.’

“‘Share trading is itself gambling,’ I said.

“‘Ordinary gambling would be preferable. Is this not enormous gambling?’ he groaned.

“I said: ‘True, Mudaliar. The telegram came to me, and no one else in town knows. My nephew knows the size of your holding, so the secret reached him first. Sell that curse to somebody.’

“He asked me to find a buyer.”

Seenu asked, “And you agreed?”

“Do you think me a fool? I said, ‘How can I oppose truth, Mudaliar? To knowingly tell a lie...’

“He begged, ‘Do me one favour, that is enough. Do not tell anyone that Lakshmiraj shares have fallen.’ I agreed.”

“Splendid! You concluded the matter with tremendous skill.”

“So I shall stop the car. Get down, take the bus coming opposite and go directly to Mudaliar. The affair will succeed. Say you have not seen me for three days.”

The car stopped and Seenu was set down. It started again.

“Where to, home or club?” Moses asked.

“Home. Moses, how many children do you have?” the Iyer asked.

“Three,” Moses replied briefly.

“You are father to three children and still refuse to abandon gambling. How can you prosper while possessing a gambler’s mind?”

The Iyer resumed his sermon. Moses repeatedly used the horn to release his anger.

“Listen to one thing, Moses. What can a gambler earn? Even suppose money comes from it. Is living by gambling a proper livelihood?”

The Iyer explained the evils of gambling. Bored and angry, Moses did not even murmur agreement.

“Moses, do my words not enter your ears?” the Iyer asked.

With a smile Moses replied, “I am not deaf. I hear everything you say very clearly.”

The Iyer realized Moses was angry, but did not know why.

Had Moses not listened while the man who condemned gambling proudly described to Seenu the gamble he himself had conducted? How could he avoid anger when such a gambler for profit called the servants’ card play for diversion and a little excitement “gambling”?

Was Moses deaf, that the conversation between Seenu and Muthusami Iyer would not reach him? He heard it clearly. But he could not speak. He was not mute; he was a servant.

How could he state the truth that his master was a first-class gambler, not with cards such as spades and clubs, but with shares named Lakshmiraj, Kamadhenu and Kantha as his cards?

12 January 1947
